# Code Standards and Best Practices

## General Principles

### Code Quality

- Production-level code quality at all times
- Modular, reusable components
- Single Responsibility Principle
- DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- SOLID principles

### Documentation Standards

- No decorative elements in code or comments
- Clear, concise inline comments for complex logic
- JSDoc/TSDoc for all public functions and classes
- README files for each major module
- API documentation using OpenAPI/Swagger

## TypeScript Standards

### Type Safety

- Strict mode enabled
- No `any` types unless absolutely necessary
- Explicit return types for all functions
- Interface-first design
- Use type guards for runtime type checking

### Naming Conventions

- Files: kebab-case (e.g., `scraping-agent.ts`)
- Classes: PascalCase (e.g., `ScrapingAgent`)
- Interfaces: PascalCase with 'I' prefix (e.g., `IScrapingAgent`)
- Functions: camelCase (e.g., `analyzeSiteStructure`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`)
- Private members: camelCase with underscore prefix (e.g., `_privateMethod`)

### File Organization

```
src/
├── agents/              # AI agents
├── api/                 # API routes and controllers
├── config/              # Configuration files
├── database/            # Database models and migrations
├── middleware/          # Express middleware
├── services/            # Business logic services
├── utils/               # Utility functions
├── types/               # Type definitions
├── validators/          # Input validation schemas
└── index.ts            # Application entry point
```

